#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# list_plot

#% text
# `list_plot([y1, y2, ...])` draws the points (1, y1), (2, y2), ... as an image.

#% text
# `list_plot([[x1, y1], [x2, y2], ...])` draws the points (x1, y1), (x2, y2), ....

#% text
# `list_plot([data1, data2, ...])` draws several data sets, each in its own colour.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The value is `image("path")`, a PNG file, with an SVG and a PDF copy of the same plot next to it; `export_data` writes it to a file.

#% item
# A list of pairs is read as points; a list of other lists is read as several data sets.

#% item
# Points whose coordinates are not real numbers are left out.

#% item
# Options:

#% subitem
# `joined: true` joins the points by a line.

#% subitem
# `x_label: "text"` and `y_label: "text"` label the axes; `legend: ["text", ...]` names the data sets.

#% subitem
# `colors: ["#rrggbb", ...]` sets the colour of each data set.

#% subitem
# `x_scale` and `y_scale`: `linear`, `log`, or `signed_log`.

#% subitem
# `filling: axis` fills the region between the points and the x axis.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# Values at x = 1, 2, 3, ...:

#% cell
list_plot([1, 4, 9, 16, 25])
#% output
#= image("list_plot.files/list_plot-1.png")

#% text
# Points given by their coordinates:

#% cell
list_plot(table([x, sin(x)], x, range(0, 6, 0.2)))
#% output
#= image("list_plot.files/list_plot-2.png")

#% text
# A cloud of random points:

#% cell
seed(4); list_plot(random_variate(normal_distribution(), [200, 2]))
#% output
#= image("list_plot.files/list_plot-3.png")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Two data sets:

#% cell
list_plot([table(k, k, 10), table(10 - k, k, 10)], legend: ["up", "down"])
#% output
#= image("list_plot.files/list_plot-4.png")

#% text
# A point that is not numeric is left out:

#% cell
list_plot([[1, 1], [2, a], [3, 3]])
#% output
#= image("list_plot.files/list_plot-5.png")

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# The points joined by a line:

#% cell
list_plot(table([x, sin(x)], x, range(0, 6, 0.2)), joined: true)
#% output
#= image("list_plot.files/list_plot-6.png")

#% text
# Labels on the axes:

#% cell
list_plot(table(k^2, k, 10), x_label: "k", y_label: "k squared")
#% output
#= image("list_plot.files/list_plot-7.png")

#% text
# A logarithmic y axis makes exponential growth a straight line:

#% cell
list_plot(table([x, exp(x)], x, range(0, 5, 0.5)), y_scale: log)
#% output
#= image("list_plot.files/list_plot-8.png")

#% text
# A colour and a filling to the axis:

#% cell
list_plot(table(k^2, k, 10), colors: ["#e45649"], filling: axis)
#% output
#= image("list_plot.files/list_plot-9.png")

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The samples of a two-parameter random walk, drawn as a scatter plot of its points:

#% cell
seed(11); chain = nest_list(p => p + 0.15*random_variate(normal_distribution(), 2), [1, 1], 1000); list_plot(chain)
#% output
#= image("list_plot.files/list_plot-10.png")

#% text
# A computed table, saved as an image file:

#% cell
export_data("squares.png", list_plot(table(k^2, k, 10)))
#% output
#= "squares.png"

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A list of pairs is read as points, not as two data sets of two values each:

#% cell
list_plot([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
#% output
#= image("list_plot.files/list_plot-12.png")

#% text
# An empty list has nothing to draw:

#% cell
list_plot([])
#% output
#! failure(argument, list_plot, 1)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `list_line_plot`, `plot`, `list_log_plot`, `list_log_log_plot`, `histogram`, `export_data`
